RISC3D test kit 19 (prototype, 13 November 2026)
================================================

RISC3D 0.9: for TORCS, which ran slower through RISC3D than in software.
TORCS draws each object of the track as many triangle strips from one set
of arrays in its own memory. RISC3D 0.8's library sent every strip all of
the arrays up to its last vertex, so most of each object was sent once
for every strip: tens of megabytes a frame. 0.9 sends only the vertices
each strip uses. The new package riscos-risc3d 0.8-1 (rol-3d 0.8) also
hands those arrays to Linux's Mesa the way a Linux program does, instead
of making a new buffer object for each one; the Track test measures both
ways.

TORCS itself only gets 0.9's library when riscos-mesa rebuilds its
!MesaLibs with it (they have been asked). This kit measures the pattern
on its own, and watches Linux while TORCS loads, to find out what makes
TORCS's loading slower and slower.

Needs
-----
 * RISC OS on Linux on the Pi 4 with riscos-kernel-pi 6.6.78-9 (the GPU's
   driver), as for the kits before.
 * A 16 million colour mode.

Steps
-----
 1. Copy RISC3DTest to HardDisc4 (unzip it there, replacing kit 18).
 2. In RiSH's Root shell:
      apt-get install /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/riscos-risc3d_0.8-1_arm64.deb
    It starts the new rol-3d itself; the modules are the same as kit 18's,
    so no restart is needed.
 3. In RiSH's Root shell:
      sh /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/linux-tests
    It runs the tests with Linux's Mesa directly, to compare with; the
    last lines are the track drawn directly.
 4. Double-click Track. TrackResults has three "track:" lines:
      1. with 0.8's library (as TORCS has now),
      2. 0.9's library, arrays each in a buffer object of their own (0.8's
         renderer),
      3. 0.9's library, arrays given to Mesa as memory (the default now).
    Compare the frames a second with TrackLinuxResults. All the "track
    picture" lines should be the same number. The "textures" lines show
    TORCS's way of loading textures, timed in groups of 50: they should
    not get slower.
 5. Double-click Info, GL3, Target, ES, Cube, Test and GL as before, to
    check that nothing went backwards.
 6. TORCS's loading: in RiSH's Root shell
      sh /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/watch 900 &
    then start TORCS (the RISC3D build you have) and load the race as
    before. Every 5 seconds for 15 minutes, WatchResults gets Linux's
    memory, swap and busiest processes. It stops by itself.

Please send
-----------
TrackResults, TrackLinuxResults and WatchResults, and a word on whether
the rest are as in kit 18. If anything stops, /riscos/3d/log.txt (the
helper's log).
